.\" Manual page for bbq-predictor.
.\"
.\" No version number appears here on purpose: VERSION is the single
.\" place this project keeps one, and a second copy in a file nobody
.\" rebuilds is a copy that goes stale silently.
.TH BBQ-PREDICTOR 1 "2026-09-04" "bbq-predictor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bbq\-predictor \- weather applet and forecast\-verification archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bbq\-predictor
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B bbq\-predictor \-\-fetch\-once
.RB [ \-\-station
.IR ID ]
.RB [ \-\-geocode
.IR LAT , LON ]
.RB [ \-\-history\-path
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B bbq\-predictor \-\-shot
.I FILE
.RI [ render
.IR options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bbq\-predictor
draws high\-resolution temperature and rain graphs from several
providers at once and answers whether an afternoon is worth lighting a
fire for.
.PP
It keeps a permanent archive of what was observed and what was forecast,
scores each provider against the measurements as they arrive, and
corrects later forecasts by the bias it has measured. The archive is the
point: a forecast is only worth what its record says it is worth, and
that record cannot be borrowed from anybody else.
.PP
With no options it runs as a tray applet where a system tray exists, and
as a plain window where none does. Closing the window puts the applet
away when there is a tray to put it in, and quits when there is not.
.PP
Everything else documented here is a diagnostic rather than a feature.
They exist because layout and data questions in this project are settled
by looking at real output, not by reasoning about it.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS General
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-station " ID"
The watched station, for example
.BR ISTOCK822 .
This is the station being looked at, which is a different idea from a
.I pinned
station: pinning is a per\-station flag in the archive that buys a
station the sparing backfill and nothing else.
.TP
.BI \-\-geocode " LAT,LON"
Where the forecast bands are aimed. Derived from the watched station
when omitted.
.TP
.BI \-\-history\-path " FILE"
Use this archive instead of the default one. For tests, for reading an
archive without opening it in the applet, and for
.B \-\-seed\-verification,
which refuses to run without it.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.SS Fetching and reporting
.TP
.B \-\-fetch\-once
Fetch every band once, archive what arrives, verify what can be
verified, report what happened, and exit. This is what the systemd timer
runs.
.TP
.B \-\-probe
Report what this machine can reach and what its TLS resolved to, then
exit. Runs before any widget is built, so it works headless. On Android,
where there is no command line, touching
.I files/run\-probe
in the application's data directory asks for the same thing; the marker
is removed as it is read, so a diagnostic cannot become a mode nobody
remembers turning on.
.TP
.B \-\-history
Report what the archive holds for the watched station and exit, without
fetching. The only way to see whether the archive is growing without
waiting a month to discover that it is not.
.TP
.B \-\-stations
List the stations the archive knows, marking the pinned ones.
.TP
.B \-\-discover
Look up the stations near the geocode and report them.
.TP
.BI \-\-search " QUERY"
Look up places matching
.I QUERY
and report their coordinates.
.SS Rendering
.TP
.BI \-\-shot " FILE"
Fetch, render the window to a PNG, and exit. Looking at the picture is
how layout defects in this project actually get found.
.TP
.BI \-\-layout " L"
.BR auto ,
.BR desktop ,
or
.BR mobile .
.TP
.BI \-\-size " WxH"
Render at a given size, for reproducing a defect seen on a device.
.TP
.BI \-\-view " SPAN[,FROM]"
Set the visible time span in seconds, optionally placing the left edge
at an absolute moment. Interaction is the one thing a screenshot cannot
exercise by itself, so this is how a zoomed or panned graph gets checked
the same way every other layout question is.
.TP
.BI \-\-cursor " N"
Park the readout on column
.IR N .
.TP
.BI \-\-interp " M"
Interpolation:
.BR step ,
.BR linear ,
.BR monotone ,
.BR akima ,
.BR makima ,
.BR natural ,
or
.BR catmull .
.TP
.BI \-\-smooth " SECONDS"
Round sample times to this many seconds.
.TP
.B \-\-wind
Show the wind series.
.TP
.BI \-\-tray\-icon " FILE"
Also save the tray icon.
.SS Seeding
.TP
.BI \-\-seed\-verification " SPEC"
Write synthetic verification statistics, so the corrected band can be
exercised before a month of real weather has gone by.
.IP
It
.B refuses
to run without
.BR \-\-history\-path .
The whole value of the archive is that everything in it was measured,
and a diagnostic that could quietly put invented numbers there would
destroy that for the sake of a screenshot.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B \-\-fetch\-once
distinguishes a partial fetch from a failed one, because a station going
quiet for an hour is ordinary and the other bands still land in the
archive:
.TP
.B 0
Every band answered.
.TP
.B 3
Some band failed, but the composite still covers now. The archive
advanced. The systemd timer forgives this by name.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing covers now: the run stored nothing usable.
.TP
.B 2
A configuration error.
.PP
Other modes exit 0 on success and non\-zero otherwise.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/bbq-predictor/bbq-predictor.ini
Settings, including the watched station. Preferences only.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/bbq-predictor/history.sqlite
The archive: observations, pending forecasts, verification sums and the
station list. Records of what was measured, kept apart from preferences
on purpose.
.TP
.I /etc/default/bbq-predictor
Read by the systemd service.
.B BBQ_STATION
must be set there; unset, the service skips its run and says why rather
than fetching for a station nobody named.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/bbq-predictor-fetch.timer
Runs
.B \-\-fetch\-once
every five minutes, so the archive advances and verification accumulates
without the window being open. The service it triggers carries no
.B [Install]
section: the timer is what starts it.
.SH NOTES
The Weather Underground key is scraped from that site rather than
licensed. Asking more often than a station reports spends somebody
else's quota for nothing, which is why the timer runs at the station's
own cadence and why a day the archive already holds whole is not fetched
again.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
.PP
.I /usr/share/doc/bbq-predictor/project.md
is the project's design record and is authoritative over this page.
